<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philaretos Brachamios</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philaretos Brachamios was an Armenian general in Byzantine service who, after the defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Manzikert</a> in 1071, built an independent principality in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. At its height it included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, Edessa, Melitene and Marash. He nominally recognised the Byzantine emperors but lost his lands to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> by the late 1080s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philaretos-brachamios.jpg" alt="Philaretos Brachamios" width="320" height="146" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philaretos_Brachamios_Lead_Seal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Some Armenian leaders set themselves up as lords; the most successful, the former Byzantine general <b>Philaretos Brachamios</b>, held between 1078 and 1085 a principality from Malatia to Antioch and from Cilicia to Edessa; it crumbled before his death in 1090.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Philaretos Brachamios</b> was an Armenian officer in Byzantine service.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-philaretos-brachamios">Principality of Philaretos Brachamios</a></li></ul></section>
